#!/bin/sh
#
# script/fatal-risks: does design/fatal-risks.md still agree with the records it cites?
#
# PROVISIONAL NAME. See the `Name:` block at the end of this comment.
#
# `design/fatal-risks.md` is the falsification list: nine claims that, if false, mean the project
# should stop. Its own BUGS section has said since the day it was written that **nothing gates this
# file**, and on 2026-09-10 a maintainer reading it against the tree found that warning firing four
# separate ways in one pass. Milestone 275 is that finding turned into a check.
#
# The defect class, stated once: every risk entry is prose ABOUT a milestone or a decision, and the
# thing it is about has a recorded status somewhere else. `script/roadmap --check` proves a cited
# `milestone N` resolves to SOME row and `script/decisions --check` proves a `§N` resolves to SOME
# decision; `script/citations` proves a gloss names the RIGHT one. **None of the three asks whether
# what this file SAYS about what it cited is still true**, which is why risk 2 could carry
# `RUN, 2026-08-30. AMBER` for eleven days while milestone 191's own block read `NOT-STARTED`, and
# why risk 9 could cite milestone 164 as the reason x86_64 has no `fs_server` nine days after 164
# turned BUILT.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is checked, and which of the 2026-09-10 findings each one answers
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **1. A risk whose experiment has run names a milestone that started.** A risk section carrying
#    `**Status: RUN ...**` or `**Status: MEASURED ...**` asserts that its experiment happened. The
#    experiment is named on a bolded lead-in the `**The experiment:**` form, naming a milestone, and a
#    milestone that is `NOT-STARTED` cannot be the thing that ran. This is the milestone-191 finding.
#
# **2. A risk whose experiment has NOT run names a milestone that is not finished.** The mirror, and
#    it is the one that goes stale silently: `**The decisive experiment that has not been run:**
#    milestone 168` is a claim that stops being true the day 168 turns BUILT, and nothing else in the
#    tree would notice. Same shape as `script/roadmap`'s rule that a `Gate: MILESTONE N` may not name
#    a BUILT milestone, which is where the idea comes from.
#
# **3. The running order's struck rows.** A row in the running-order table whose order is struck
#    (`~~1~~`) or whose cost cell reads `done` says that experiment is finished, so its owner
#    milestone may not be `NOT-STARTED`. The milestone-191 finding again, from the second direction,
#    because the file records that result in two places and they drifted together.
#
#    **The other direction is deliberately NOT checked**, and the reason is a false positive this
#    would otherwise produce today. Row 5's owner is milestone 85 (mutation testing), which is BUILT,
#    while the row's experiment ("re-run the mutation sweep against the baseline") is genuinely
#    pending: the owner column names who owns the experiment, not a milestone whose completion IS the
#    experiment. So a pending row over a BUILT owner is legitimate and stays unreported.
#
# **4. A stated status word must be the recorded one.** `milestone 161 is `PARTIAL`` and
#    `§86 is DECIDED` are the cheapest claims in the file to check and the cheapest to get wrong.
#    Exact match against `script/roadmap`'s and `script/decisions`' own vocabulary.
#
# **5. A cited path exists**, including the proposal files, since a proposal cited as pending work
#    stops existing the day it is promoted to a numbered milestone.
#
# **6. A risk entry may not be older than a status change it depends on** (the "as of" check, and
#    the only one here that is about time rather than about contradiction). Every risk section states
#    dates; the latest one is when somebody last looked. A milestone cited in that section whose
#    recorded **Built date is after** that day finished after the entry was last revised, so what the
#    entry says about it was written without knowing. This is the milestone-164 finding, and it is
#    the only check here that would have caught it: risk 9 never states 164's status, it reasons from
#    what 164 says, and no status-word comparison can see that.
#
#    **Milestones use the roadmap's own recorded Built date; a cited proposal uses git.** The
#    asymmetry is not tidy and it is honest: a proposal has no completion date to record, so the only
#    available answer to "did this move after the risk entry did" is the last commit that touched it.
#    That is what catches the risk-6 finding, where the instrument
#    (`design/roadmap/proposals/time-the-hw-entropy-step.md`) shipped on 2026-09-10 and the entry
#    still called the step untimed. Exactly one proposal is cited in the file today, so the noise
#    surface of the git half is one path rather than the tree.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What is deliberately NOT checked
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **A premise that was overtaken.** Risk 9's second stale claim was that milestone 177 needs the
# graphical stack, which stopped holding when `DECISIONS §149` landed. No status field encodes a
# premise, and nothing here pretends to see one. Milestone 275's block says so in its own BUGS and
# this says so where the reader meets the check: **a green run means no status word contradicts the
# record, never that the file's arguments still hold.**
#
# **And the tuning is asymmetric on purpose**, because the costs are. A false negative here is one
# more thing a person has to notice, which is where this file already was. A false positive is worse
# than the drift: `design/fatal-risks.md` is meant to be read and trusted, and a gate that reddens on
# a status that moved harmlessly teaches the next lane to reach for an allow-list. So every check
# above is anchored on an explicit marker the file already writes, and a citation with no marker
# makes no claim and is not judged.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where it runs
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# **In `script/lint`, on every push**, beside `script/roadmap --check` and `script/citations`.
# `script/cadence-check` is the nearby precedent for the other answer and its header draws the line
# this sits on the far side of: a dead cadence "is not a defect in the commit that happens to trip
# it", so blocking an unrelated lane on it is unfair. Drift here is the opposite. It is *caused* by
# an in-tree edit, always: either a milestone's status moved, or this file was written against a tree
# that has changed. The commit that trips it is the commit that caused it, and the person holding it
# can fix it in the same breath, which is the definition of a lint. The cost is about a second, most
# of it the two report subprocesses this reuses rather than reimplements.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# How this is known to be able to fail
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# `script/fatal-risks --selftest` drives every check above against a fixture built to trip it, and
# **requires each one to come back red**. It is the same discipline DECISIONS §134 put on a Kani
# harness (a proof that has never been falsified is a claim about a claim) applied to a gate, and it
# is here for a defect this tree has already had twice: `script/roadmap`'s merged-branch check
# "spent a day passing in CI while being unable to fail", and milestone 193's record is that both of
# its claims were "falsified before they were believed".
#
# The fixtures are in `SELFTEST` at the bottom. A check that stops firing on its own fixture is a
# check that has quietly died, which `--check` alone could never tell you, because on a clean tree a
# dead check and a working one both print the same green line.
#
# **One fixture is for a check that refuses rather than answers.** A shallow clone has no commit
# touching a tracked file, so the proposal check would pass vacuously in one; it reports instead.
# That is script/roadmap's lesson taken whole, from the day its merged-branch check "spent a day
# passing in CI while being unable to fail" for exactly this reason.
#
# **Three of the fixtures are negative**, and they are the more interesting half, because this
# script's stated bias is that a false positive costs more than a false negative. They pin the three
# near-misses the real file produces today: a PARTIAL milestone is evidence for either claim and must
# fire on neither; a pending running-order row over a BUILT owner is legitimate (row 5 owns risk 3 to
# milestone 85, which is BUILT, while its experiment is a re-run that has not happened); and a
# milestone built before a risk entry's own date is what the entry was written knowing.
#
# # EXAMPLES
#
#     script/fatal-risks             # report every claim this file makes, classified
#     script/fatal-risks --check     # validate; non-zero on a disagreement (wired into script/lint)
#     script/fatal-risks --selftest  # prove every check can still come back red
#
# # BUGS
#
# - **It cannot see a premise going stale**, per the section above. That is the larger half of what
#   the 2026-09-10 review found by hand and it stays a person's job.
# - **The "as of" check reads a section's latest date, which is a proxy for when somebody last
#   looked.** A lane that fixes a typo in a risk entry without touching a date leaves the proxy
#   correct; one that adds a date while changing nothing else silences a real finding. The proxy is
#   the file's own convention (every verdict in it is dated) rather than something invented here, but
#   it is a proxy.
# - **A risk entry that cites nothing makes no checkable claim.** Risk 8 ("nobody needs it") is the
#   worked example and is not a defect: it is the one entry with no experiment.
# - **It reports, and cannot fix.** Every finding names the two records that disagree and leaves
#   which one is wrong to the reader, because that is the half a script cannot know.
# - **The selftest proves each check can fire, not that it fires on everything it should.** A fixture
#   is one worked example per check. The prose shapes it does not recognise (a risk that states a
#   milestone's condition without its status word, which is how risk 9 cited milestone 164) are
#   caught by the "as of" check or by nobody, and the fixtures cannot tell you which.
#
# Name: provisional. Minted 2026-09-11 by milestone 275's lane. The stem is the record it reads,
# which is what DECISIONS §134's noun half asks of a `script/` entry that reports (`citations`,
# `roadmap`, `decisions`, `names`), and the hyphen is the `script/` domain's own spelling. Refused
# `risk-check`, because "risk" names a risk register and this file's first paragraph exists to say
# it is not one; refused `falsifications-check`, which collides with `script/falsifications` over a
# different sense of the word (a harness's replayable falsification record, DECISIONS §134); and
# refused `fatal-risk-drift`, which names the symptom and reads as a report of drift rather than a
# gate against it. calef has not ratified it.

set -e

ROOT="$(CDPATH='' cd -- "$(dirname -- "$0")/.." && pwd)"

exec python3 - "$ROOT" "${1:-}" <<'PY'
import os
import re
import subprocess
import sys

root, mode = sys.argv[1], sys.argv[2]
RISKS = "design/fatal-risks.md"

# A milestone status that means work happened, and one that means it is over. The split matters in
# both directions: `NOT-STARTED` is the only token that can contradict "this ran", and BUILT/REMOVED
# are the only ones that can contradict "this has not run yet". PARTIAL sits in neither, which is
# deliberate and is most of what keeps this quiet: a PARTIAL milestone is simultaneously a thing that
# ran and a thing with work left, so it is honest evidence for either claim.
STARTED = ("BUILT", "PARTIAL", "REMOVED", "IN-PROGRESS")
FINISHED = ("BUILT", "REMOVED")

CITE_M = re.compile(r"(?<![a-zA-Z])[Mm]ilestone (\d+)")
CITE_D = re.compile(r"§(\d+)")
DATE = re.compile(r"\b(\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2})\b")
SECTION = re.compile(r"^## (?:(\d+)\. )?(.+)$", re.M)

# The bolded lead-in that names a risk's experiment, and whether it says the experiment is pending.
# Anchored on `**` and on the word "experiment" so an ordinary sentence mentioning an experiment is
# not read as a declaration. The `not been run` arm is what makes the pending claim explicit rather
# than inferred from the absence of a status line, which is the difference between a check and a
# guess.
EXPERIMENT = re.compile(r"\*\*The [a-z ]*experiment\b([^*:]*)(?::\*\*|\*\*)", re.I)
STATUS_LINE = re.compile(r"\*\*Status: ([A-Z]+)\b")
PATHISH = re.compile(r"`((?:design|notes|script|scripts|crates|kernel|user|xtask)/[A-Za-z0-9_./-]+"
                     r"\.(?:md|rs|sh|toml|yml))`")
PROPOSAL = re.compile(r"`(design/roadmap/proposals/[A-Za-z0-9_./-]+\.md)`")

# A sentence ends at a period followed by whitespace, unless the period is inside a filename. That
# guard is not hypothetical: risk 2's status paragraph opens `notes/proof-retrospective.md has the
# study`, and without it the sentence ends after `notes/proof-retrospective`.
SENTENCE_END = re.compile(r"(?<!\.md)(?<!\.rs)(?<!\.sh)(?<!\.yml)(?<!\.toml)(?<!\.log)\.(\s)")


def first_sentence(s):
    m = SENTENCE_END.search(s)
    return s[:m.start()] if m else s


def sections_of(text):
    """`## N. title` through the next `## `. The running order and BUGS are `##` sections without a
    leading number, so they cannot be mistaken for a risk that has no experiment."""
    out, heads = [], list(SECTION.finditer(text))
    for i, h in enumerate(heads):
        end = heads[i + 1].start() if i + 1 < len(heads) else len(text)
        out.append((h.group(1), h.group(2), h.start(), text[h.start():end]))
    return out


def analyse(text, milestones, built_on, decisions, exists, last_touched):
    """Every disagreement between what `text` says and what the records hold.

    The records arrive as arguments rather than being read in here, which is what lets `--selftest`
    drive each check against a fixture. A checker that can only be run against the real tree is a
    checker whose ability to fail cannot be demonstrated, and this tree has already shipped one of
    those (script/roadmap's merged-branch check, green for a day inside a shallow clone that made
    it unable to fail at all).

    Returns a list of (code, line, message). The code is what the selftest asserts on; the message
    is what a person reads.
    """
    found = []

    def lineno(pos):
        return text.count("\n", 0, pos) + 1

    def report(code, pos, msg):
        found.append((code, lineno(pos), msg))

    sections = sections_of(text)
    risks = [s for s in sections if s[0]]
    if not risks:
        found.append(("no-risks", 1, f"{RISKS}: no numbered risk sections found. Has the shape "
                                     f"of the file changed?"))
        return found

    for num, title, start, body in risks:
        label = f"risk {num}"
        sm = STATUS_LINE.search(body)
        verdict = sm.group(1) if sm and sm.group(1) in ("RUN", "MEASURED") else None

        # ---- 1 and 2: the experiment -----------------------------------------
        for em in EXPERIMENT.finditer(body):
            # The lead-in's own sentence, and only that. Risk 6's `**The decisive experiment:** one
            # real, non-virtio device on real silicon, confined, at throughput.` is followed in the
            # SAME paragraph by "The JH7110's GMAC (milestone 53) ... are the candidates", which
            # names candidates rather than the experiment. A paragraph-wide read would take 53 as
            # the experiment and be wrong about which milestone the claim is about.
            sentence = first_sentence(body[em.end():em.end() + 400])
            cited = [int(m.group(1)) for m in CITE_M.finditer(sentence)]
            if not cited:
                continue
            pending = bool(re.search(r"not (yet )?(been )?run|unrun|has not", em.group(1), re.I))
            n = cited[0]
            if n not in milestones:
                continue
            got = milestones[n]
            if verdict and not pending and got not in STARTED:
                report("experiment-ran", start + em.start(),
                       f"{RISKS}:{lineno(start + em.start())}: {label} says its experiment is "
                       f"{verdict}, and names milestone {n}, which the roadmap records as {got}.\n"
                       f"      A milestone that never started is not the thing that ran. Either the "
                       f"work landed and {n}'s status was never moved (the case this check was "
                       f"written for: milestone 191 sat NOT-STARTED for twelve days after pull "
                       f"request #589 merged its study), or this entry names the wrong milestone.")
            if pending and got in FINISHED:
                report("experiment-pending", start + em.start(),
                       f"{RISKS}:{lineno(start + em.start())}: {label} says its experiment has not "
                       f"been run, and names milestone {n}, which the roadmap records as {got}.\n"
                       f"      The same rule script/roadmap keeps for a `Gate: MILESTONE N`: the "
                       f"day the milestone lands, a claim that it is still pending fails rather "
                       f"than quietly staying stale.")

        # ---- 4: a stated status word -----------------------------------------
        for m in re.finditer(r"[Mm]ilestone (\d+) (?:is|was) \*{0,2}`?([A-Z][A-Z-]+)`?", body):
            n, claimed = int(m.group(1)), m.group(2)
            if n in milestones and claimed in set(milestones.values()) and milestones[n] != claimed:
                report("status-word", start + m.start(),
                       f"{RISKS}:{lineno(start + m.start())}: {label} says milestone {n} is "
                       f"{claimed}; the roadmap records {milestones[n]}")
        for m in re.finditer(r"§(\d+) (?:is|was) \*{0,2}`?([A-Z][A-Z-]+)`?", body):
            n, claimed = int(m.group(1)), m.group(2)
            if n in decisions and claimed in set(decisions.values()) and decisions[n] != claimed:
                report("status-word", start + m.start(),
                       f"{RISKS}:{lineno(start + m.start())}: {label} says §{n} is {claimed}; "
                       f"the decision index records {decisions[n]}")

        # ---- 6: the entry is older than a status change it depends on --------
        dates = DATE.findall(body)
        asof = max(dates) if dates else None
        if not asof:
            continue
        for n in sorted({int(m.group(1)) for m in CITE_M.finditer(body)}):
            built = built_on.get(n)
            if built and built > asof:
                report("as-of", start,
                       f"{RISKS}:{lineno(start)}: {label} was last dated {asof} and cites "
                       f"milestone {n}, which the roadmap records as "
                       f"{milestones.get(n, '?')} on {built}.\n"
                       f"      The milestone finished after this entry was last revised, so what "
                       f"the entry says about it was written without knowing. Read it, and answer "
                       f"in this file's own idiom: a dated correction paragraph saying what changed "
                       f"and what it does or does not do to the risk.")
        # ---- 6b: a cited proposal that moved after the entry did -------------
        # Git rather than a recorded date, because a proposal has no completion to record. Scoped to
        # the proposals this file cites, which is one path today, so the noise surface of the git
        # half is a path rather than the tree.
        for rel in dict.fromkeys(m.group(1) for m in PROPOSAL.finditer(body)):
            if not exists(rel):
                continue
            touched = last_touched(rel)
            if not touched:
                # **A history this cannot see makes this check pass vacuously**, and that is the
                # exact failure script/roadmap records against its own merged-branch check: it
                # "spent a day passing in CI while being unable to fail" inside an
                # `actions/checkout` shallow clone, and a check that cannot fail is worse than one
                # that is absent, because its green is read as an answer. A tracked file with no
                # commit touching it is a shallow clone, not a file nobody has ever written.
                report("no-history", start + body.index(rel),
                       f"{RISKS}:{lineno(start + body.index(rel))}: `{rel}` is cited by risk {num} "
                       f"and this git history has no commit touching it, so the check that asks "
                       f"whether it moved after the entry did cannot answer.\n"
                       f"      That is a shallow clone (actions/checkout defaults to depth 1), not "
                       f"a file nobody has written. Give the job `fetch-depth: 0`.")
                continue
            if touched > asof:
                report("proposal-moved", start + body.index(rel),
                       f"{RISKS}:{lineno(start + body.index(rel))}: risk {num} was last dated "
                       f"{asof} and cites `{rel}` as work that would answer it; that proposal was "
                       f"last changed {touched}.\n"
                       f"      A proposal cited as an instrument that does not exist yet is a claim "
                       f"about the tree, and it goes stale the day the instrument merges (the case "
                       f"this check was written for: risk 6 called the hw-entropy step untimed "
                       f"after the tour began timing it). Read the proposal and re-date the entry.")

    # ---- 3: the running order table ------------------------------------------
    # One direction only. A row that is NOT marked finished over a BUILT owner is legitimate and
    # stays unreported: row 5 owns risk 3 to milestone 85, which is BUILT, while its
    # experiment is a re-run that has not happened, because the owner column names who owns the
    # experiment rather than a milestone whose completion IS the experiment.
    order = next((b for n, t, st, b in sections if t.lower().startswith("the running order")), "")
    base = text.index(order) if order else 0
    for row in order.split("\n"):
        cells = [c.strip() for c in row.strip().strip("|").split("|")]
        if len(cells) != 5 or cells[0] == "order" or set(cells[0]) <= set("-"):
            continue
        struck = cells[0].startswith("~~")
        done = cells[4].lower().startswith("done")
        owner = CITE_M.search(cells[3])
        if not owner or not (struck or done):
            continue
        n = int(owner.group(1))
        if n in milestones and milestones[n] not in STARTED:
            report("running-order", base + order.index(row),
                   f"{RISKS}:{lineno(base + order.index(row))}: the running order marks risk "
                   f"{cells[1].split(',')[0]}'s experiment finished (order {cells[0]}, cost "
                   f"{cells[4]!r}) and owns it to milestone {n}, which the roadmap records as "
                   f"{milestones[n]}.\n"
                   f"      A struck row and a NOT-STARTED owner cannot both be true.")

    # ---- 5: a cited path exists ----------------------------------------------
    for m in PATHISH.finditer(text):
        if not exists(m.group(1)):
            report("missing-path", m.start(),
                   f"{RISKS}:{lineno(m.start())}: cites `{m.group(1)}`, which does not exist. A "
                   f"proposal cited as pending work stops existing the day it is promoted to a "
                   f"numbered milestone, and a note cited as evidence stops existing when it moves")

    return found


# ---- the two records, from the scripts that own them --------------------------
# Reused rather than reimplemented, which is the point: `script/roadmap --check` has already proved
# the index row and the milestone's own file agree by the time anything here runs, so there is one
# derivation of "what is milestone N's status" in this tree and this is not a second one.
def record(script, pattern, broken):
    out = subprocess.run([os.path.join(root, "script", script)],
                         capture_output=True, text=True, cwd=root)
    if out.returncode != 0:
        broken.append(f"script/{script} would not run, so nothing here can be checked:\n"
                      f"      {out.stderr.strip()[:200]}")
        return {}
    status, table = None, {}
    for line in out.stdout.splitlines():
        head = re.match(r"^([A-Z][A-Z -]+) \(\d+\)$", line)
        if head:
            status = head.group(1).strip()
            continue
        m = re.match(pattern, line)
        if m and status:
            table[int(m.group(1))] = status
    return table


def built_dates():
    """The Built column, which is the one fact the report does not carry and the "as of" check
    needs. `script/roadmap --check` already enforces this column's shape (five fields, a date on
    every BUILT and REMOVED row and on no other), so this reads a validated column rather than
    deriving a status of its own."""
    out = {}
    for line in open(os.path.join(root, "design", "roadmap", "README.md"), errors="replace"):
        cells = [c.strip() for c in line.strip().strip("|").split("|")]
        if len(cells) == 5 and cells[0].isdigit() and re.fullmatch(r"\d{4}-\d{2}-\d{2}", cells[4]):
            out[int(cells[0])] = cells[4]
    return out


def git_touched(rel):
    return subprocess.run(["git", "-C", root, "log", "-1", "--format=%ad", "--date=short",
                           "--", rel], capture_output=True, text=True).stdout.strip() or None


# ---- the selftest ------------------------------------------------------------
# One fixture per check, each a minimal `design/fatal-risks.md` and a minimal record set. Positive
# fixtures must fire the named code; negative ones must fire nothing, and they pin the three
# near-misses the real file produces today.
MILE = {7: "BUILT", 8: "NOT-STARTED", 9: "PARTIAL"}
DATES = {7: "2026-09-09"}

SELFTEST = [
    ("experiment-ran", "a risk whose experiment RAN over a NOT-STARTED milestone",
     "## 1. A risk\n\n**The experiment:** milestone 8.\n\n**Status: RUN, 2026-09-10.**\n"),
    ("experiment-pending", "a risk whose experiment has NOT run over a BUILT milestone",
     "## 1. A risk\n\n**The decisive experiment that has not been run:** milestone 7.\n"),
    ("status-word", "a status word this file spells out that the roadmap contradicts",
     "## 1. A risk\n\nmilestone 9 is `BUILT`, which is the sort of thing that rots.\n"),
    ("as-of", "a risk entry last dated before a milestone it cites turned BUILT",
     "## 1. A risk\n\nDated 2026-09-01, and it reasons from what milestone 7 says.\n"),
    ("running-order", "a struck running-order row owned to a NOT-STARTED milestone",
     "## 1. A risk\n\n## The running order\n\n| order | risk | experiment | owner | cost |\n"
     "|---|---|---|---|---|\n| ~~1~~ | 1, a risk | **RUN.** | milestone 8 | done |\n"),
    ("missing-path", "a cited path that does not exist",
     "## 1. A risk\n\n`design/roadmap/proposals/gone.md` is the instrument that would.\n"),
    ("proposal-moved", "a cited proposal changed after the entry was last dated",
     "## 1. A risk\n\nAs of 2026-09-01, `design/roadmap/proposals/here.md` is what would fix it.\n"),
    # ---- the negatives, which are the more interesting half ------------------
    ("no-history", "a git history too shallow to answer whether a cited proposal moved",
     "## 1. A risk\n\nAs of 2026-09-01, `design/roadmap/proposals/mute.md` is what would fix it.\n"),
    (None, "a RUN verdict over a PARTIAL milestone is evidence for either claim",
     "## 1. A risk\n\n**The experiment:** milestone 9.\n\n**Status: RUN, 2026-09-10.**\n"),
    (None, "a pending running-order row over a BUILT owner is legitimate (the milestone-85 shape)",
     "## 1. A risk\n\n## The running order\n\n| order | risk | experiment | owner | cost |\n"
     "|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 5 | 1, a risk | re-run the sweep | milestone 7 | a day |\n"),
    (None, "a milestone built before the entry's own date is what the entry knew",
     "## 1. A risk\n\nDated 2026-09-10, and it reasons from what milestone 7 says.\n"),
]


def selftest():
    bad = []
    for want, why, fixture in SELFTEST:
        got = analyse(fixture, MILE, DATES, {},
                      lambda rel: rel in ("design/roadmap/proposals/here.md",
                                          "design/roadmap/proposals/mute.md"),
                      lambda rel: None if rel.endswith("mute.md") else "2026-09-09")
        codes = sorted({c for c, _, _ in got})
        ok = (codes == [want]) if want else (codes == [])
        print(f"  {'ok  ' if ok else 'FAIL'}  {want or '(quiet)':<20} {why}")
        if not ok:
            bad.append(f"{why}: wanted {[want] if want else []}, got {codes}")
    print()
    if bad:
        for b in bad:
            print(f"fatal-risks: selftest: {b}", file=sys.stderr)
        print("\nA check that cannot come back red is worse than no check, because its green is "
              "read as an answer.", file=sys.stderr)
        return 1
    print(f"fatal-risks: {len(SELFTEST)} fixtures, every check still able to fail and every "
          f"near-miss still quiet")
    return 0


if mode == "--selftest":
    sys.exit(selftest())

broken = []
MILESTONES = record("roadmap", r"^\s+(\d+)\s+\S", broken)
DECISIONS = record("decisions", r"^\s+§(\d+)\s", broken)
text = open(os.path.join(root, RISKS), errors="replace").read()
problems = [(None, 1, b) for b in broken] + analyse(
    text, MILESTONES, built_dates(), DECISIONS,
    lambda rel: os.path.exists(os.path.join(root, rel)), git_touched)

risks = [s for s in sections_of(text) if s[0]]
if mode == "--check":
    for _, _, msg in problems:
        print(f"fatal-risks: {msg}", file=sys.stderr)
    if problems:
        print("", file=sys.stderr)
        print("design/fatal-risks.md is the list of claims that would stop this project, so it is "
              "read", file=sys.stderr)
        print("and trusted. Each finding names the two records that disagree; which one is wrong is "
              "the", file=sys.stderr)
        print("reader's call. A green run means no STATUS WORD contradicts the record, never that "
              "the", file=sys.stderr)
        print("file's arguments still hold. See design/roadmap/275-fatal-risks-status-check.md.",
              file=sys.stderr)
        sys.exit(1)
    cited_m = {int(m.group(1)) for m in CITE_M.finditer(text)}
    cited_d = {int(m.group(1)) for m in CITE_D.finditer(text)}
    print(f"fatal-risks: {len(risks)} risks, {len(cited_m)} milestones and {len(cited_d)} decisions "
          f"cited, every stated status agrees with the record it names")
    sys.exit(0)

if mode:
    print(f"fatal-risks: unknown argument {mode!r} (no argument, --check, or --selftest)",
          file=sys.stderr)
    sys.exit(2)

print(f"{len(risks)} risks in {RISKS}\n")
for num, title, start, body in risks:
    sm = STATUS_LINE.search(body)
    dates = DATE.findall(body)
    cited = sorted({int(m.group(1)) for m in CITE_M.finditer(body)})
    shown = ", ".join(f"{n}:{MILESTONES.get(n, '?')}" for n in cited) or "--"
    print(f"  risk {num:<2} {title[:58]}")
    print(f"          verdict {sm.group(1) if sm else '--':<9} as of {max(dates) if dates else '--'}")
    print(f"          cites   {shown}")
if problems:
    print(f"\nPROBLEMS ({len(problems)})")
    for _, _, msg in problems:
        print(f"  {msg}")
else:
    print("\nno disagreement between a stated status and the record it names")
